Q: ASP.NET mobile page not redirecting for customErrors A: <p>You're using redirectMode=&quot;ResponseRewrite&quot;, which, unlike a regular redirect, tries to do a Server.Transfer to keep the URL same. If an error happens in a sub directory a relative path in defaultRedirect=&quot;/Error/Error.aspx&quot; might end up being resolved incorrectly or the Rewrite might fail because of directory depth.  Try changing redirectMode to ResponseRedirect temporarily.  If the redirect immediately starts working, the issue is the way the server is trying to rewrite the path. Additionally, in your logs, there is: Method: LogAsync. If your btn_submit_Click is async void or calling async incorrectly, it could escape the standard WebForms cycle. customErrors depends on the synchronous page lifecycle. If an exception gets thrown on a background thread the Global.asax will log it maybe, but the response has already been broken. and customErrors won't know how to handle the redirect. Since your Global_asax is successfully catching the error in the logs, the best workaround is to manually force the redirect. Modify your Application_error in Global.asax.cs like so:</p>
<pre><code>
    LogAsync(&quot;Global_asax - Unhandled Exception&quot;, exc);


    if (Context.IsCustomErrorEnabled)
    {
        Server.ClearError(); 
        Response.Redirect(&quot;~/Error/Error.aspx&quot;);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>The reason it isn't redirecting now is that in your logs, Global.asax handles the error, but the execution continues on to the ASP.NET handler because Server.ClearError() never got called.</p>

Q: Using number as &quot;index&quot; (JSON) A: <p>First off, it's not JSON: JSON mandates that all keys must be strings.</p>
<p>Secondly, regular arrays do what you want:</p>
<pre><code>var Game = {
  status: [
    [
      &quot;val&quot;,
      &quot;val&quot;,
      &quot;val&quot;
    ],
    [
      &quot;val&quot;,
      &quot;val&quot;,
      &quot;val&quot;
    ]
  }
</code></pre>
<p>will work, if you use <code>Game.status[0][0]</code>. You cannot use numbers with the dot notation (<code>.0</code>).</p>
<p>Alternatively, you can quote the numbers (i.e. <code>{ &quot;0&quot;: &quot;val&quot; }...</code>); you will have plain objects instead of Arrays, but the same syntax will work.</p>

Q: Shrink DIV to text that&#39;s wrapped to its max-width? A: <p>It's not the prettiest solution but it should do the trick. The logic is to count the length of each word and use that to work out what the longest line is that will fit before being forced to wrap; then apply that width to the div. Fiddle here: <a href="http://jsfiddle.net/uS6cf/50/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">http://jsfiddle.net/uS6cf/50/</a></p>
<p>Sample html...</p>
<p>And the javacript (relying on jQuery)</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>$.fn.fixWidth = function() {
  $(this).each(function() {
    var el = $(this);
    // This function gets the length of some text
    // by adding a span to the container then getting it's length.
    var getLength = function(txt) {
      var span = new $("&lt;span /&gt;");
      if (txt == ' ')
        span.html('&amp;nbsp;');
      else
        span.text(txt);
      el.append(span);
      var len = span.width();
      span.remove();
      return len;
    };
    var words = el.text().split(' ');
    var lengthOfSpace = getLength(' ');
    var lengthOfLine = 0;
    var maxElementWidth = el.width();
    var maxLineLengthSoFar = 0;
    for (var i = 0; i &lt; words.length; i++) {
      // Duplicate spaces will create empty entries.
      if (words[i] == '')
        continue;
      // Get the length of the current word
      var curWord = getLength(words[i]);
      // Determine if adding this word to the current line will make it break
      if ((lengthOfLine + (i == 0 ? 0 : lengthOfSpace) + curWord) &gt; maxElementWidth) {
        // If it will, see if the line we've built is the longest so far
        if (lengthOfLine &gt; maxLineLengthSoFar) {
          maxLineLengthSoFar = lengthOfLine;
          lengthOfLine = 0;
        }
      } else // No break yet, keep building the line
        lengthOfLine += (i == 0 ? 0 : lengthOfSpace) + curWord;
    }
    // If there are no line breaks maxLineLengthSoFar will be 0 still. 
    // In this case we don't actually need to set the width as the container 
    // will already be as small as possible.
    if (maxLineLengthSoFar != 0)
      el.css({
        width: maxLineLengthSoFar + "px"
      });
  });
};

$(function() {
  $(".fixed").fixWidth();
});</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>.wrapper {
  text-align: center;
  background-color: #eee;
  overflow: auto;
  padding: 20px 0;
}

.shrunken {
  display: inline-block;
  font-size: 24px;
  background-color: #ddd;
  max-width: 130px;
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/jquery/2.0.0/jquery.min.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;div class="wrapper"&gt;
